WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:08.073
What game can be played on the computer, blindfolded, by mail, and in a room?

2
00:00:08.794 --> 00:00:11.796
What game has a 1,500-year-old history?

3
00:00:12.456 --> 00:00:18.440
What game has been played by kings, philosophers, monks, and everyday people in countries all over the world?

4
00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.984
The answer is chess━a game, a sport, and a mental workout all in one!

5
00:00:24.744 --> 00:00:29.807
Chess is a board game played between 2 people on a square board of 64 black and white

6
00:00:29.847 --> 00:00:36.101
squares. Each player begins the game with 16 pieces, and each piece can be moved and used to

7
00:00:36.142 --> 00:00:37.822
capture other pieces on the board.

8
00:00:38.522 --> 00:00:46.645
The pieces are called pawns (8 per player), knights (2), bishops (2), rooks (2), queens (1), and kings

9
00:00:46.865 --> 00:00:52.906
(1). One player controls all the white pieces; the other player controls all the black pieces.

10
00:00:53.606 --> 00:00:56.427
The object of the game is to capture your opponent's king.

11
00:00:57.368 --> 00:01:03.554
The History of Chess Chess is reputed to be the world's most popular game and is currently played

12
00:01:03.614 --> 00:01:07.136
recreationally and competitively by millions around the world.

13
00:01:07.737 --> 00:01:13.080
The origins of chess are contentious, but it seems that chess was invented in India in the sixth

14
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.060
century AD.

15
00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:21.684
The Sanskrit word for chess, chaturanga, means "four parts" because chess was modeled on an Indian army platoon,

16
00:01:21.964 --> 00:01:28.288
which had four parts─elephants, chariots, soldiers on horseback, and foot soldiers─as well as a king.

17
00:01:28.924 --> 00:01:34.708
In ancient India, chess was a gambling game played with four players and dice, but when the game

18
00:01:34.748 --> 00:01:38.631
spread to Persia, modern-day Iran, it quickly developed further.

19
00:01:39.491 --> 00:01:46.316
The Persians translated the old Indian names into Persian, reduced the number of players to two, discarded the

20
00:01:46.356 --> 00:01:50.699
dice, and added a new piece, an advisor, which would later become the queen.

21
00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:54.241
Chess became a game of strategy and intelligence.

22
00:01:55.402 --> 00:02:00.941
The most important word in a game of chess is "checkmate"; it means that a player's king can't

23
00:02:01.001 --> 00:02:03.403
escape capture, so the game is over.

24
00:02:04.203 --> 00:02:08.926
Interestingly, this word comes from the Persian word shah-mat, or "king-help."

25
00:02:09.586 --> 00:02:15.689
The Persians didn't have time to make any more changes to chess because the Arabs invaded in 614

26
00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:22.353
AD. In Persia, the Arabs took up chess and were very serious players, with chess professionals employed at

27
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:27.576
court. In the seventh century, the Arabs conquered Spain and chess came to Europe.

28
00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:34.532
Chess became popular throughout the continent, but there was great confusion concerning the names of the pieces, and

29
00:02:34.572 --> 00:02:35.773
again the game changed.

30
00:02:36.393 --> 00:02:43.237
The elephant piece became an archer in Spain, a messenger in Germany, and a court jester in France.

31
00:02:43.397 --> 00:02:49.541
In 1580, an Italian made the radical suggestion that the queen become the strongest piece in the game,

32
00:02:49.901 --> 00:02:51.082
rather than the weakest.

33
00:02:51.842 --> 00:02:53.964
The game underwent major changes again.

34
00:02:54.696 --> 00:02:59.518
The names of the pieces were standardized, and the average chess game was now played in half the

35
00:02:59.558 --> 00:03:01.319
time it used to take to play.

36
00:03:01.339 --> 00:03:06.562
This new game was called Crazy Chess by the Italians and Chess of the Angry Queen by the

37
00:03:06.602 --> 00:03:12.685
French. Eventually, it became the norm, and the rules of this version are the rules that modern chess

38
00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:13.805
players use today.

39
00:03:14.526 --> 00:03:19.368
Until the twentieth century, chess was only played by the wealthy and highly educated.

40
00:03:20.052 --> 00:03:25.155
Today, it is played in parks, clubs, libraries, and homes by people of all classes.

41
00:03:25.995 --> 00:03:30.877
In Russia, chess is considered a national sport and is more popular than soccer.

42
00:03:31.518 --> 00:03:36.060
In fact, Russian chess players have been the best in the world since the 1940s.

43
00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:44.064
The Computer Revolution The twentieth century has also seen a revolution in chess with the advent of computer

44
00:03:44.084 --> 00:03:52.401
technology. In 1985, a Chinese doctoral student named Feng-hsiung Hsu, who was studying in the U.S., began to

45
00:03:52.461 --> 00:03:55.323
construct a chess-playing computer called Chiptest.

46
00:03:56.084 --> 00:03:58.345
This computer was further developed by Mr.

47
00:03:58.365 --> 00:04:02.849
Hsu and IBM, and 12 years later, was re-launched as Deep Blue.

48
00:04:03.549 --> 00:04:10.514
The team behind Deep Blue designed a chess-specific processor chip that is capable of examining and evaluating two

49
00:04:10.574 --> 00:04:13.036
to three thousand chess positions per second.

50
00:04:13.832 --> 00:04:20.717
In 1996, Deep Blue played its first six-game match against the world chess champion, Garry Kasparov.

51
00:04:21.378 --> 00:04:26.661
Although the computer won the first game, Kasparov won the match by winning three games and drawing two.

52
00:04:27.242 --> 00:04:32.866
The chess computer Hydra is the intellectual descendant of Deep Blue and is designed to be able to

53
00:04:32.926 --> 00:04:34.187
beat any human player.

54
00:04:34.827 --> 00:04:40.972
In June 2005, Hydra scored a decisive victory over the world's seventh-best player, Michael Adams.

55
00:04:41.872 --> 00:04:49.294
The Great Chess Automaton In the eighteenth century, chess fans around the world were also captivated by another

56
00:04:49.354 --> 00:04:52.775
chess-playing machine─the Great Chess Automaton.

57
00:04:53.595 --> 00:04:59.517
The Automaton Chess Player was invented in 1769 by Baron Kempelen of Hungary.

58
00:04:59.537 --> 00:05:05.939
It consisted of a wooden figure dressed in Turkish clothes whose upper body was attached to a wooden

59
00:05:05.979 --> 00:05:07.819
box filled with gears and wires.

60
00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:14.856
The machine was wound up, and the figure would play against human opponents by moving its mechanical arms.

61
00:05:15.857 --> 00:05:20.380
The Automaton Chess Player was an excellent player and won almost every match.

62
00:05:21.161 --> 00:05:27.306
It toured Europe and America and even beat Benjamin Franklin, the brilliant American writer and scientist!

63
00:05:27.886 --> 00:05:32.670
Before each show, the machine was opened to show that a real person was not hidden inside.

64
00:05:33.631 --> 00:05:35.873
Still, most people were skeptical.

65
00:05:36.673 --> 00:05:43.107
The secret was revealed seventy years after its invention: the cleverly constructed box could, in fact, hide a

66
00:05:43.167 --> 00:05:48.272
full-size man (not a dwarf, as some people thought), who was a chess champion.

67
00:05:49.653 --> 00:05:55.659
Chess Around the World There are many variants of chess around the world, with the most popular being

68
00:05:55.719 --> 00:05:59.863
xiangqi in China, shogi in Japan, and janggi in Korea.

69
00:06:00.584 --> 00:06:04.768
Janggi is played on a board that is nine lines wide by ten lines long.

70
00:06:05.488 --> 00:06:10.731
Each player has 16 pieces, green and red, and their names are written in Chinese characters.

71
00:06:11.491 --> 00:06:17.155
Some character names are similar to the names used in ancient Persian chess; for example, the elephant and

72
00:06:17.195 --> 00:06:17.815
the chariot.

73
00:06:18.555 --> 00:06:23.578
Unlike in Western chess, where the game is concentrated in the few middle rows for most of the

74
00:06:23.598 --> 00:06:27.380
game, in janggi the game takes place all over the board.

75
00:06:27.961 --> 00:06:33.664
Janggi is also slower than Western chess, with professional games often lasting over one hundred moves.

76
00:06:34.708 --> 00:06:42.851
Chess has featured in popular culture, literature, customs, politics, science, technology, and even religion throughout its long history.

77
00:06:43.412 --> 00:06:46.053
There are some interesting trivia connected to this game.

78
00:06:46.793 --> 00:06:53.235
The first chess game played by telephone was played by two gentlemen in England, in 1878.

79
00:06:53.396 --> 00:07:01.479
In 1922, the world chess champion, Jose Capablanca, played 103 opponents at the same time in Cleveland, U.S.A.,

80
00:07:02.019 --> 00:07:04.200
and drew one game and won all the rest.

81
00:07:04.936 --> 00:07:10.259
And the first chess match played between a player in space and one on Earth took place in

82
00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:16.903
1970. Astronauts on a Russian spacecraft played their ground crew on a chess set designed specifically for the

83
00:07:16.943 --> 00:07:17.984
weightless environment.

84
00:07:18.624 --> 00:07:21.726
The game of chess has spread across all of the world's oceans.

85
00:07:22.466 --> 00:07:27.169
In fact, it has been said, "There are more adventures on a chessboard than on all the seas

86
00:07:27.209 --> 00:07:27.789
of the world."
